if it's triggered multiple times like from both the keyword match handler and the wake-up function, we could run into problems. The `removeTap` call should be safe since it doesn't throw, but I should add a guard flag to prevent redundant cleanup attempts. I'll keep the implementation close to the spec but add that protection.\n\nNow I need to handle the AlarmRingView side—adding a task property to manage the speech recognition lifecycle, canceling it properly in the wake-up handler, and accounting for any errors that might be thrown during the listening startup.\n\nSetting up AudioRecorder... Setting up the audio recorder with the configured settings and URL, then starting the recording while tracking the state and current file location. The stop function simply halts recording and updates the flag. Now I'm moving on to build the RecordingView interface, which needs to bind to the alarm model so it can save the recording name after capture.\n\nBuilding the recording interface... Writing the UI components... I'm noticing a potential issue with the preview — since `RecordingView` uses `@Bindable` to bind to an `Alarm` model, the preview might run into problems because `@Bindable` expects the model to be properly initialized within a SwiftData container context. a `@Model` requires a model context. Let me think about this...\n\nActually, for `@Bindable` to work with SwiftData `@Model`, the model needs to be in a context. The preview uses `.modelContainer(for: Alarm.self, inMemory: true)` but the `Alarm` instance in the preview isn't in that container. This is the same `sampleAlarms @Model without ModelContext` issue that's been noted in the ring.\n\nFor the preview, I could use a different approach. But looking at the existing AlarmEditorView preview pattern, it creates the Alarm directly - for RecordingView, the Alarm is passed in.\n\nLooking at the AlarmListView that uses `@Bindable var alarm: Alarm` - it has the same pattern. The `SampleAlarmCard` avoids this by using a non-model approach. For RecordingView, since it's launched from AlarmEditorView which already has a real model, the production path is fine. The preview won't work cleanly with `@Bindable` on a model-less instance, but this is a known limitation already noted in the ring - I'll just follow the same pattern used in AlarmListView.\n\nNow for RewardView, I'm adding a confetti animation. I'll create individual star elements that animate from the top of the screen downward with varying delays and positions, using opacity to fade them out as they fall.\n\nWriting confetti animation... Writing confetti particle system... Writing the confetti particle data... Building the sparkle animation... Defining the icon array...\n\nNow I need to add some tests as requested. I should write at least two test cases—one for `AudioRecorder` to verify the file URL construction works correctly, and another for `SpeechRecognizer` to check the initial state and keyword matching logic.\n\nAdding test cases...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the navigation flow—AlarmEditorView saves the alarm but doesn't navigate to RecordingView, and RecordingView needs an existing Alarm to update the recording name. I need to figure out how to connect these two views so that after creating or editing an alarm, the user can navigate to record audio for it. I'm going to keep this straightforward: after the user saves the alarm in AlarmEditorView, I'll store a reference to it and then show a button to navigate to RecordingView with that saved alarm object. This avoids needing a full edit flow and keeps the implementation simple. I'm settling on the simpler approach: keep AlarmEditorView focused on just saving the alarm, then add a recording button to AlarmCard in the list view so users can tap an alarm and access the recording feature from there. a minimal change to AlarmEditorView that keeps the existing dismiss behavior intact—I'll just add a NavigationLink to RecordingView that appears after the alarm is saved, so users can optionally record audio without disrupting the current flow. Modifying AlarmEditorView to keep a reference to the saved alarm and show a recording option afterward seems like the cleanest approach—I'll add state to track the saved alarm and a navigation link to RecordingView that only appears after the alarm is created. doesn't break the existing UX: I'll modify `saveAlarm()` to set a flag after scheduling, then show a recording option before dismissing. This keeps the flow in AlarmEditorView without forcing it.\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering—it makes more sense to keep AlarmEditorView focused on just creating the alarm, then let users add recordings from the alarm list itself. I'll add a microphone button to `AlarmCard` that opens the recording view as a sheet, so the natural flow is: create alarm → see it listed → optionally tap to record. This way I'm not changing the existing editor behavior.\n\nAdding the recording button to AlarmCard... I'm laying out the full plan for the remaining changes: I need to create the audio recorder and recording view components, update the alarm list to include recording functionality, add visual feedback with confetti to the rewards screen, and write tests to cover the new features. Let me start by examining the current AlarmListView to see where the recording button should go.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1967,"cache_read_input_tokens":51346,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1967,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3514461e-587c-4525-adfa-cd83cf29b320","uuid":"24c7fda2-5b63-4883-b4fe-af990fa66c30","request_id":"req_011CbaNBn2GAPo7okbRWhRWa"}
